[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc11, pc12
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: end-host IP configuration on the Location C access segment (switch5). Both PC11 and PC12 are configured with the same Layer-3 address.

Mechanism: With two hosts advertising 192.168.200.12 on the same L2 domain, ARP resolution for 192.168.200.12 oscillates between two different MAC addresses (pc11 eth0 5e:0a:cf:c4:5d:9c and pc12 eth0 82:20:62:b8:6e:ac). Neighbors (including the default gateway 192.168.200.1 and other PCs) keep updating their ARP cache to whichever host last replied, so traffic destined to 192.168.200.12 gets delivered alternately to PC11 or PC12. As their inbound traffic “flaps,” both hosts experience intermittent connectivity, and pings to “PC12” sometimes hit the wrong machine and fail.

Localization: Location C access switch (switch5) ports:
- pc11 on cd_sw5_fa1_1 (switch5.eth1), IP 192.168.200.12/24
- pc12 on cd_sw5_fa2_1 (switch5.eth2), IP 192.168.200.12/24
Direct evidence: get_host_net_config shows both pc11 and pc12 have inet 192.168.200.12/24 with default via 192.168.200.1.

Root cause: Duplicate statically assigned IP address 192.168.200.12 configured on both PC11 and PC12.

Corrective fix: Assign unique addresses. For example, set pc11 to 192.168.200.11/24 and keep pc12 at 192.168.200.12/24 (or per your IP plan). After changing, restart networking or down/up eth0, and send a gratuitous ARP on each host to update neighbors (e.g., arping -A -I eth0 <new-IP>). Optionally flush stale ARP entries on the default gateway. This will stop ARP MAC flapping and restore stable connectivity. 
=== END ===